WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.028 --> 00:00:06.440
Last week, police in
Washington State made an arrest

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.940
in the 30-year-old killing
of a Canadian couple.

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:12.370
She had been sexually assaulted

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:14.370
and shot in the back of the head.

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:16.313
He had been beaten and strangled.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:19.760
There was plenty of DNA evidence,

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.493
but nobody really to point a finger at.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.700
For 31 years there was
basically no resolution

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.560
but then the power of using genetics

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:36.720
and genealogy was able to
get the name of a suspect

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:37.773
within a day.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.820
Because of this sort of
analysis we can now do,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.240
you can discover things
about the relatives of people

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.120
who have uploaded their genetic
information to the internet

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.070
and I think that's what's
both the power of this method

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:55.070
and also the thing that raises a lot

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:58.210
of these disquieting
questions about privacy

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.783
and consent, that we're
going through right now.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:12.913
We're evolving

00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:15.143
how DNA shows who you're connected to.

00:01:16.990 --> 00:01:19.010
Genetics is this thing that we think of

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:20.750
as cutting edge science, you know?

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:24.170
It's happening in some
of the most advanced labs

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:25.080
in the world.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.170
And genealogy is something that, you know,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:29.880
maybe makes you think
of your grandparents.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:32.670
You know, looking up records,
maybe going to churches

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:35.400
and looking at gravestones to kind of try

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.210
and trace your family tree back.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:39.960
So genetic genealogy is what happens

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.333
when you combine those two sciences.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:44.380
A family tree represents

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:45.843
so much more than history.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:52.160
So in the mid-2000s, this
new industry started to grow

00:01:53.277 --> 00:01:58.238
based on the rise of an of a
consumer DNA testing industry.

00:01:59.113 --> 00:02:01.620
And that was something that
came out of the sequencing

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:02.730
of the human genome,

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.560
basically companies trying to figure out

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.520
how to make this a consumer product.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.740
I decided to have my DNA
tested through Ancestry DNA.

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.543
The big surprise was
we're not German at all.

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.950
Family Tree DNA was the
first company on the scene.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:23.430
They were really targeting
hardcore genealogists,

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:26.090
and then you have companies
that are more targeted

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:28.010
at a broader commercial audience,

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.423
start coming into the scene.

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:34.390
Now, GEDmatch really was
an important breakthrough

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:35.470
in genetic genealogy

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.570
because what it did was it
created an open source site

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.890
where if you had done Ancestry's DNA test,

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:45.390
you could also compare
your results to people

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.920
who had done other DNA tests.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.280
But, you know, my DNA isn't just my DNA,

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.430
it's my cousin's DNA, it's my mom's DNA,

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:56.600
and so when I make a
decision to share my DNA

00:02:56.600 --> 00:03:00.428
I'm also making a decision
to share their DNA.

00:03:00.428 --> 00:03:03.540
So, at this point,

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:06.770
at least 30 million people have taken

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.893
these consumer DNA tests.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.460
Some large portion of those people

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:15.280
have also uploaded the results
to these public databases

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.153
that are searchable by
anyone who's curious.

00:03:20.130 --> 00:03:23.740
30 million might not seem
like a big number to you,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:27.320
of course, you know there's
billions of people in the world,

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.820
but the reason that number
is significant is that

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.810
you don't necessarily have to have shared

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.520
your genetic information in
order for your information

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.550
to be exposed.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.760
So, there was one study that found

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:46.460
that just 2% of people in
the world would need to share

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.120
their genetic information

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.556
for us to potentially be
able to identify everybody.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.780
I'm joined right now
with genetic genealogist,

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.520
that's a mouthful right there, CeCe Moore.

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:02.930
Hi, how are you?
Great to meet you.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.410
I'm happy to be here.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:06.690
So CeCe Moore is probably one

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:10.160
of the most interesting people
that I have ever interviewed.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:14.443
She started off her career
as a model, actress.

00:04:15.342 --> 00:04:19.210
At one point, she made
a living playing Barbie.

00:04:19.210 --> 00:04:21.030
And at one point she decided

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:25.140
that she wanted to make a family tree

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:28.569
as a gift for her niece
who was getting married.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.160
This sort of became

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.090
this pretty all-consuming pastime for her.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:40.810
And she didn't have, you know,
formal scientific training,

00:04:40.810 --> 00:04:42.920
it's just that she was
doing a lot of this.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:45.892
And through sort of trial and error,

00:04:45.892 --> 00:04:48.800
she realized the way that you could merge

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.750
these traditional genealogical methods

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:54.490
with the genetic information

00:04:54.490 --> 00:04:56.550
and also

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.860
social media.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:02.080
She came up with this idea of
triangulating your relatives

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:05.790
to discover the hidden
identity of someone.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:08.370
And then she became
the genetic genealogist

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.690
on this TV show called
"Finding Your Roots."

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:12.880
So take a look at this family tree.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:15.730
This is Christian Kummel
and Theresa Stockhausen.

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.950
We traced another of their descendants

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:19.760
and found one of their great-grandsons

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:22.070
who was willing to test for us.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.590
So as CeCe Moore is
helping all these adoptees

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:26.940
find their birth parents,

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:29.240
people begin asking the question of

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:32.760
what else can we use this technique for?

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:37.470
And there's one really obvious answer

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.550
to what else you can use that for

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:43.113
and that is figuring out
who committed a crime.

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.370
June 18th, 1976 marked
the first known rape

00:05:48.370 --> 00:05:50.770
by the East Area Rapist.

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:52.060
The Golden State Killer

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:55.720
was this serial murder-rapist

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:58.360
who terrorized California in the '70s.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:00.480
There was plenty of DNA evidence,

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.040
but nobody really to point a finger at

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:06.030
so it became a cold case.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:10.230
And then in the spring of 2018,

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:12.740
there was this announcement of the solving

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:17.450
of this decades-old case
that had used these methods

00:06:17.450 --> 00:06:20.707
to basically identify the suspect.

00:06:20.707 --> 00:06:22.800
CeCe Moore sees this as a green light.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:26.510
She gets in contact with
this company called Parabon,

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:29.700
which can take really degraded DNA samples

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:32.600
and extract information out of them

00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:36.300
and make the same sort of genetic profile,

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.840
the same kind of file that
you would get from a 23andMe

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.840
or an Ancestry.

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.040
She had already been in contact

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.070
with a few police departments

00:06:45.070 --> 00:06:47.050
and detectives across the country,

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:51.850
including detectives in
Snohomish County, Washington

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:55.463
where there had been a very brutal murder.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:06:59.030
21-year-old Jay Cook

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.030
and 18-year-old Tanya Van
Cuylenborg were murdered

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:05.380
while visiting Seattle from
Victoria, British Columbia.

00:07:05.380 --> 00:07:06.860
There were a few clues.

00:07:06.860 --> 00:07:08.460
There was this blue blanket

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:10.850
that the one of the bodies
had been wrapped in.

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:15.500
And there was DNA and 1987
happens to be the very dawn

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.820
of the DNA analysis-era
in law enforcement.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:23.480
But there are really
severe limitations to that.

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.410
It's really more of a confirmation tool

00:07:26.410 --> 00:07:29.010
than like a true detective tool.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:31.440
And even as these databases grew,

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:32.830
there weren't any matches.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.480
This should tell us which ones

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:36.560
we can be more confident about.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:39.040
CeCe gets to work doing her part,

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:42.160
which is trying to figure out

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.380
what kinds of records
and stuff you can use

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.970
to build out this family tree.

00:07:45.970 --> 00:07:50.020
Not every crime scene DNA
sample will have a relative

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:52.650
that has uploaded their
genetic information.

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:57.260
So you kind of have to win
the lottery and CeCe won big.

00:07:57.260 --> 00:08:00.380
She didn't just get
one match, she got two.

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:03.090
So basically, whoever gave that sample,

00:08:03.090 --> 00:08:07.770
the suspect has two relatives
in the GEDmatch database.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:10.090
And they're pretty good matches

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.860
in the context of genetic genealogy,

00:08:12.860 --> 00:08:14.300
they're second cousins.

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.710
And when she built out the family tree,

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:19.560
there was only one male

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:22.350
that was the right amount of relatedness

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:24.163
to the genetic hits on GEDmatch.

00:08:25.221 --> 00:08:27.720
And they knew from other evidence

00:08:27.720 --> 00:08:28.830
that they were looking for a male,

00:08:28.830 --> 00:08:32.410
so it was a very strong
indication, very quickly,

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:34.900
of who their suspect might be.

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:36.730
The suspect happened to be a truck driver,

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:40.050
so they followed him around
in his truck for a few days

00:08:40.050 --> 00:08:43.800
and at one point, a coffee
cup falls out of the truck

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:46.340
when he's pulling away
from a traffic light

00:08:46.340 --> 00:08:48.730
and they are able to test the saliva

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.713
on the lip of the coffee
cup and it matches.

00:08:53.860 --> 00:08:55.310
Yesterday, we took into custody

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:58.270
a 55-year-old SeaTac man who is suspected

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:02.860
of the 1987 murders of Jay
Cook and Tanya Van Cuylenborg.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:04.250
We, the jury, find the defendant,

00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:07.070
William Earl Talbott
II, guilty of the crime

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:10.049
of first degree murder
as charged in the count.

00:09:10.960 --> 00:09:15.240
It's an incredible testament
to the power of these tools,

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:16.850
given the right kind of data,

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:20.360
someone with CeCe's abilities is able

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:23.340
to sift through an
enormously large data set

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:25.223
and come back with a single name.

00:09:27.650 --> 00:09:30.260
So to go from entertainment

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:33.560
and then end up in a
science career in my 40s

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.030
is definitely not something
I would've ever imagined.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:37.770
Without genetic genealogy,

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.010
this case never would have been solved.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:43.740
CeCe Moore did in two hours

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:48.343
what 20 or 30 cops
couldn't do in 30 years.

00:09:49.540 --> 00:09:52.730
I think most people
don't realize the power

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:53.930
of these types of tests.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:57.880
When this happened, there
was sort of this agreement,

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:01.710
okay, we can use this
very powerful technology

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:03.430
to solve cold cases.

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:08.430
But very quickly, people
started to use the technology

00:10:08.435 --> 00:10:13.435
for other things, for murders
that were not long unsolved.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:16.820
And I think when that happened,

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:21.390
that started to raise the
level of discomfort people had.

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:24.060
The particularly sticky thing here

00:10:24.060 --> 00:10:28.270
is that you're not just
sharing your information,

00:10:28.270 --> 00:10:30.620
you're sharing your family's information.

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:33.410
And that's a tough thing,
especially when we know things

00:10:33.410 --> 00:10:35.920
like the criminal justice system

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:39.610
disproportionately
targets minorities, right?

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:43.313
Or when we know that sometimes
DNA evidence is wrong.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:59.340
And I think another part of
the cautionary aspect of this

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:00.623
is that

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:07.140
this is an application of genetic analysis

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.910
that nobody dreamed of

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:11.920
when we set out

00:11:11.920 --> 00:11:15.220
to sequence the human genome.

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.280
And it's only through this combination

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:18.850
of these obsessive people

00:11:18.850 --> 00:11:21.930
with their weird interests
discovering this tool

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:23.770
that we have ended up here.

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:26.200
And so I think there's bound to be

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:29.590
other totally unforeseen uses for this

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:33.760
that we can't even imagine right now

00:11:33.760 --> 00:11:36.530
Somebody might find out, you know,

00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:39.450
about your family health
history, for example.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:40.570
A life insurer,

00:11:40.570 --> 00:11:43.520
if they knew that you were
prone to heart attacks,

00:11:43.520 --> 00:11:45.140
they would be able to use that information

00:11:45.140 --> 00:11:46.770
to deny you a policy.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:48.900
So it's not just, you know,

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:53.900
crime suspects that face
exposure with this information.

00:11:54.330 --> 00:11:58.060
All of us could be exposed
in one way or another

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.370
once this information is out there.

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:02.550
It's an enormously rich data trove

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:05.260
that a lot of people have
decided to make public.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:07.750
And perhaps the people who uploaded it

00:12:07.750 --> 00:12:10.503
haven't fully thought
through the implications.

